---
title: Tom yum noodle soup
servings: 2
tags: [thai, pork]
cuisine: thai
time: 50 minutes
source: Tom Yum Noodle Soup (Kuay Teow Tom Yum) by Sarah Leung, The Woks of Life, https://thewoksoflife.com/tom-yum-noodle-soup-kuay-teow-tom-yum/
---

== Broth ==

Trim the @lemongrass{1%stalk}, cut it into 3-inch lengths and split them. Put
them in a #saucepan{} with the @chicken stock{5%cups}(or pork stock), the
@cilantro roots{5}(or stems) and the @?fish balls{6}(or 4 oz sliced fish cake).
Bring to a simmer, cover, and keep it barely bubbling while you do the rest.

== Chili vinegar ==

Stir the @Thai chiles{3}(red, chopped) into the @white vinegar{1/4%cup} and
leave at least ~{15%minutes}.

== Garlic oil (optional) ==

Cook the @?garlic{7-8%cloves}(finely chopped) in the @?neutral oil{1/4%cup}
over medium heat until just pale gold, then take it off the heat.

== Bowls ==

Bring a #large pot{} of water to the boil. Drop in the @ground pork{8%oz} and
cook until it turns opaque. Lift it out and divide it between two bowls.

Boil the @rice noodles{8%oz}(dried, thin, pho-style) in the same water until
just tender.

While they cook, put half of each of these in each bowl: @sugar{4%tsp},
@fish sauce{2%tbsp}, @lime juice{3%tbsp}, @?Thai chile flakes{1-2%tsp},
@roasted peanuts{2%tbsp}(finely chopped), @scallions{2%tbsp}(chopped) and
@cilantro{2%tbsp}(chopped).

Drain the noodles into the bowls and ladle the hot broth and fish balls over.
Spoon on a little garlic oil, stir everything together, and add chili vinegar
at the table.

> The seasoning sits in the bowl, not the pot, so taste and adjust each bowl
> once the broth is in.
